"28_point.mst — Point. Strongtalk's real Point.dlt is ABSTRACT (x/y are
 subclassResponsibility; a separate concrete HeavyPoint class actually
 holds the coordinates, and `Point class>>x:y:` returns a HeavyPoint) —
 an artifact of its typed-protocol design wanting room for multiple
 concrete point representations. MACVM has no second representation to
 make room for, so this collapses the whole thing into one concrete
 class holding x/y directly (WORLD.md's 'Layer B: reimplement fresh,
 consult originals for protocol surface').

 expandRect:/insetRect:/corner:/extent: are DELIBERATELY NOT ported — they
 return/accept a Rectangle, Strongtalk's own UI-toolkit geometry class,
 which MACVM has no use for at all: the GUI is HTML/JS-rendered
 (gui/src/objc.rs's WKWebView shell), not Smalltalk-Rectangle-based
 (WORLD.md's Layer C: 'UI, replaced by WKWebView').

 Also reopens Number (the `@` constructor operator, plus `truncateTo:`,
 needed for Point>>truncateTo: to have something to call) and Integer
 (floor/truncated/rounded as identity operations — genuinely absent
 before this file, since nothing previously needed a SmallInteger's own
 floor/truncated/rounded; Double already has all three, 08_double.mst)."

nil subclass: Object [
    isPoint ^ <Boolean> [
        ^false
    ]
]

"I represent a position or extent on a plane as an x and a y coordinate.
 Create me with the @ operator: 3 @ 4. Arithmetic (+, -, *, /, //) works
 coordinatewise against another Point, and my comparisons answer true only
 when BOTH coordinates satisfy the relation, so p < q and q < p can both
 be false. Other protocol: min:, max:, abs, negated, floor, rounded,
 truncated, truncateTo:, dist: (Euclidean distance), dotProduct:,
 translatedBy:, and relativeTo:. I print myself as x@y."
Object subclass: Point [
    | x <Number> y <Number> |

    Point class >> x: xval <Number> y: yval <Number> ^ <Point> [
        ^self basicNew setX: xval y: yval
    ]
    Point class >> origin ^ <Point> [
        ^self x: 0 y: 0
    ]
    setX: xval <Number> y: yval <Number> [
        x := xval.
        y := yval.
    ]
    x ^ <Number> [
        ^x
    ]
    y ^ <Number> [
        ^y
    ]
    isPoint ^ <Boolean> [
        ^true
    ]
    asPoint ^ <Point> [
        ^self
    ]
    hash ^ <Integer> [
        ^(x hash bitShift: 10) bitXor: (x hash bitXor: y hash)
    ]
    = other <Object> ^ <Boolean> [
        other isPoint ifFalse: [ ^false ].
        ^x = other x and: [ y = other y ]
    ]
    "The comparisons coerce for the same reason the arithmetic does; Dolphin
     writes `aPoint > 0` to mean both coordinates positive."
    < other <Object> ^ <Boolean> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^x < p x and: [ y < p y ]
    ]
    <= other <Object> ^ <Boolean> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^x <= p x and: [ y <= p y ]
    ]
    > other <Object> ^ <Boolean> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^x > p x and: [ y > p y ]
    ]
    >= other <Object> ^ <Boolean> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^x >= p x and: [ y >= p y ]
    ]
    "ARITHMETIC COERCES ITS OPERAND with `asPoint`, which is the ANSI
     contract and Dolphin's: `aPoint * 2` scales both coordinates, exactly as
     `aPoint * (2@2)` does. Written to assume a Point argument, every one of
     these raised `Class 'int' has no instance method 'x'` on a scalar — and
     the scalar spelling is the one the corpus uses. It reached DD11 through
     `IconicListAbstract>>scaledImageExtent`:

         ^Icon standardSizeFor: (self imageExtent * self dpi // 96)

     where `self dpi` is an Integer, so the whole DPI-scaling of every icon in
     every list and tree control failed. It fails on a 96-dpi display too;
     nothing about it is high-DPI-specific."
    + other <Object> ^ <Point> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^Point x: x + p x y: y + p y
    ]
    - other <Object> ^ <Point> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^Point x: x - p x y: y - p y
    ]
    * other <Object> ^ <Point> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^Point x: x * p x y: y * p y
    ]
    / other <Object> ^ <Point> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^Point x: x / p x y: y / p y
    ]
    // other <Object> ^ <Point> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^Point x: x // p x y: y // p y
    ]
    \\ other <Object> ^ <Point> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^Point x: x \\ p x y: y \\ p y
    ]
    abs ^ <Point> [
        ^Point x: x abs y: y abs
    ]
    negated ^ <Point> [
        ^Point x: x negated y: y negated
    ]
    floor ^ <Point> [
        ^Point x: x floor y: y floor
    ]
    rounded ^ <Point> [
        ^Point x: x rounded y: y rounded
    ]
    truncated ^ <Point> [
        ^Point x: x truncated y: y truncated
    ]
    truncateTo: grid <Number> ^ <Point> [
        ^Point x: (x truncateTo: grid) y: (y truncateTo: grid)
    ]
    max: other <Object> ^ <Point> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^Point x: (x max: p x) y: (y max: p y)
    ]
    min: other <Object> ^ <Point> [
        | p | p := other asPoint.
        ^Point x: (x min: p x) y: (y min: p y)
    ]
    "Dolphin's `ArithmeticValue>>strictlyPositive`, `^self > self class zero`,
     which for a Point means BOTH coordinates positive — the guard every
     bitmap-sized thing uses before allocating: `ImageList class >> extent:`
     refuses a non-positive extent, and `Icon>>isStandardSize` tests one. It
     reaches Point rather than Number because the corpus sends it to extents,
     and it works because Point's comparisons coerce (see above)."
    strictlyPositive ^ <Boolean> [
        ^self > 0
    ]
    positive ^ <Boolean> [
        ^self >= 0
    ]
    relativeTo: p <Point> ^ <Point> [
        ^self - p
    ]
    translatedBy: p <Point> ^ <Point> [
        ^self + p
    ]
    printOn: s [
        x printOn: s.
        s nextPutAll: '@'.
        y printOn: s.
    ]
]

Magnitude subclass: Number [
    @ aNumber <Number> ^ <Point> [
        ^Point x: self y: aNumber
    ]
    "The other half of Point's coercion: a scalar IS the square point. Without
     it every `aPoint * anInteger` in the corpus raised."
    asPoint ^ <Point> [
        ^Point x: self y: self
    ]
    "The scalar spellings of the same two tests Point answers above."
    strictlyPositive ^ <Boolean> [
        ^self > 0
    ]
    positive ^ <Boolean> [
        ^self >= 0
    ]
    truncateTo: grid <Number> ^ <Number> [
        ^(self / grid) truncated * grid
    ]
]

Number subclass: Integer [
    floor ^ <Integer> [
        ^self
    ]
    truncated ^ <Integer> [
        ^self
    ]
    rounded ^ <Integer> [
        ^self
    ]
]
